<header class="sg-head"><p class="featured-region">Sacred · 1876 · Americas</p><h1 class="sg-title">Deep River</h1><p class="sg-kid" data-say>An African American spiritual, first printed in 1876, that became one of the best-known spirituals sung in concert halls.</p></header>
<dl class="sg-facts"><div><dt>Words</dt><dd>anonymous</dd></div><div><dt>Music</dt><dd>anonymous</dd></div><div><dt>Culture</dt><dd>African Americans</dd></div><div><dt>Language</dt><dd>English</dd></div><div><dt>Place</dt><dd>United States</dd></div><div><dt>Date</dt><dd>first printed 1876</dd></div><div><dt>Type</dt><dd>Sacred</dd></div><div><dt>The words</dt><dd>Public domain (anonymous African American spiritual; printed 1876)</dd></div></dl>
<div class="sg-body">
<section class="sg-sec" id="story"><h2>The story</h2><p>&quot;Deep River&quot; is an anonymous African American spiritual. It was first mentioned in print in 1876, in the first edition of J. B. T. Marsh's The Story of the Jubilee Singers: With Their <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/song-dynasty">Songs</a>. Samuel Coleridge-Taylor arranged its melody as the tenth of his 24 Negro Melodies, Op. 59 (1905), and Daniel Gregory Mason quoted it in his String Quartet on Negro <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/theme-system">Themes</a>, Op. 19 (1919). Harry Burleigh made it widely known in his collection Jubilee Songs of the USA (1916); by 1917, when he finished the last of his several influential arrangements, it had become very popular in recitals. It has been called &quot;perhaps the best known and best-loved spiritual&quot;.</p><p>Its melody was taken in 1921 for the song &quot;Dear Old Southland&quot; by Henry Creamer and Turner Layton, a popular success the next year in versions by Paul Whiteman and Vernon Dalhart. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/marian-anderson">Marian Anderson</a> recorded it for Victor in November 1923 and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/paul-robeson">Paul Robeson</a> on 10 May 1927; in the film Show Boat (1929) Laura La Plante mouthed it to the singing of Eva Olivetti, and Robeson sang it with a male chorus in The Proud Valley (1940). Michael Tippett wrote it into his oratorio A Child of Our Time (1941) as one of five spirituals. Later recordings include those of Tommy Dorsey (1941), Odetta (1957), Johnny Mathis (1958), the Roger Wagner Chorale and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mahalia-jackson">Mahalia Jackson</a> (1964), Bobby Womack (2012) and Beverly Glenn-Copeland (2020).</p></section>
<section class="sg-sec" id="meaning"><h2>What it says</h2><p>An anonymous African-American spiritual, a sacred song; the account used here does not describe its words.</p></section>
<section class="sg-sec" id="words"><h2>The words</h2><p class="sg-nowords sg-nowords-g" data-say>The words of this song are in the public domain, but no free, reliable edition has been found yet to import them from, and the Institute never types a song&#8217;s words from memory: Genius shows them below, under its own licence.</p><div class="sg-genius"><div class="sg-genius-btns"><button type="button" class="sg-genius-btn" data-genius="6885629" data-what="Lyrics">Show the lyrics from Genius</button></div><div class="sg-genius-frames"></div><p class="sg-genius-note">Genius shows the lyrics in its own frame, under its own licence: nothing loads from Genius until you press a button. <a href="https://genius.com/Paul-robeson-deep-river-lyrics" target="_blank" rel="noopener">The song on Genius</a>.</p></div></section>
</div>
<section class="sg-sec" id="listen"><h2>Listen</h2><div class="sg-video" data-yt="M9IahLd25cM" data-title="Deep River: Marian Anderson, contralto"><button type="button" class="sg-play"><span class="sg-play-ic" aria-hidden="true"></span><span class="sg-play-t">Play the performance</span><span class="sg-play-n">Marian Anderson, contralto</span></button></div><p class="sg-video-cr">On YouTube, from the channel Marian Anderson - Topic. Pressing play loads YouTube&#8217;s player. <a href="https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=M9IahLd25cM" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Watch on YouTube<span aria-hidden="true"> &#8599;</span></a></p></section>
<section class="sg-sec" id="inspired"><h2>What it inspired</h2><ul class="sg-chain"><li><span class="sg-chain-n">24 Negro Melodies, no. 10 (Samuel Coleridge-Taylor)</span> <span class="sg-chain-y">1905</span><span class="sg-chain-h">arrangement</span></li><li><span class="sg-chain-n">String Quartet on Negro Themes (Daniel Gregory Mason)</span> <span class="sg-chain-y">1919</span><span class="sg-chain-h">quoted in</span></li><li><span class="sg-chain-n">Dear Old Southland (Henry Creamer and Turner Layton)</span> <span class="sg-chain-y">1921</span><span class="sg-chain-h">the same melody</span></li><li><span class="sg-chain-n">A Child of Our Time (Michael Tippett)</span> <span class="sg-chain-y">1941</span><span class="sg-chain-h">one of five spirituals in an oratorio</span></li></ul></section>
